--------------------|\n| \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) | \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-3}{2-1}=-2\\) (sunny) |\n| \\(L_{1},L_{3}\\) | \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{3-1}=-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny) |\n| \\(L_{2},L_{3}\\) | \\((1,2),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1\\) (sunny) |\n\nIn each sub‑case the two leftover points lie on a line whose slope is \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) or \\(-2\\); all three slopes are sunny. Therefore the third line would also be sunny, contradicting the requirement that **exactly** two lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B – At least one sunny line contains only a single point of \\(S_{3}\\)\n\nSuppose one sunny line, say \\(\\ell_{1}\\), passes through a single point of \\(S_{3}\\).  \nThe other sunny line \\(\\ell_{2}\\) can contain at most two points (Section 1).  \nThus the two sunny lines together cover at most three points, leaving **four** points of \\(S_{3}\\) for the third line \\(\\ell_{3}\\).\n\nBy Section 2 any non‑sunny line can contain at most three points, so \\(\\ell_{3}\\) cannot be non‑sunny.  \nConsequently \\(\\ell_{3}\\) would have to be sunny, which would give three sunny lines—again a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince both exhaustive cases lead to contradictions, no arrangement of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ the value }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
