the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); the remaining points have \\(a=n\\) and necessarily \\(b=1\\), which lie on \\(H_1\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Small case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   - The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n     \\[\n     (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n     \\]\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  The three non‑sunny lines \\(V_1,\\;H_1,\\;D_5\\) (i.e. \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\)) cover all six points.  \n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  Take the non‑sunny lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x\\); the three lines cover all points.  \n   - **\\(k=2\\).**  Any choice of two sunny lines leaves at most one non‑sunny line.  A direct case‑by‑case inspection shows that the three points not lying on that single non‑sunny line cannot be covered by only two sunny lines (any two of them are either collinear with a forbidden slope or require a third line).  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n   - **\\(k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n     \\]\n     (passing respectively through the pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\)) cover all six points, giving a configuration with three sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n4. **Partial attempts for larger \\(n\\).**  \n   - I succeeded in constructing examples with \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\) (as in step 2).  \n   - I also produced a construction with \\(k=1\\) for \\(n=4\\) (using the non‑sunny lines \\(V_1,V_2,H_1\\) together with the sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\)).  \n   - Attempts to realise \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=4\\) repeatedly ran into a combinatorial obstruction: after fixing any two non‑sunny lines, the remaining uncovered points form a set of three points that cannot be covered by only two sunny lines because any two of them are either collinear with a forbidden slope or require a third line.  This suggests that \\(k=2\\) may be impossible for \\(n=4\\), but I have not produced a general proof.  \n\n   For \\(k\\ge 2\\) and general \\(n\\) I was unable to devise a systematic method that either yields a construction or proves impossibility.  The difficulty lies in balancing the coverage of the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\) while respecting the restriction on slopes.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe problem remains unsolved in full generality.  I have established that \\(k=0\\) is always possible and have completely classified the admissible values of \\(k\\) for the minimal case \\(n=3\\).  Determining the exact set of admissible \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\ge4\\) requires further work.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
