of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The remaining points form a smaller triangle\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\ a'+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a>n-k\\), set \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\).  \nThen \\(a'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b=(a-(n-k))+b\\le (n+1-b)-(n-k)+b=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a',b)\\in T_k'\\).  \nConversely, any \\((a',b)\\in T_k'\\) corresponds to \\((a,b)=(a'+n-k,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a>n-k\\).  \nThus the points not covered by the vertical lines are exactly the translate of \\(T_k'\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines that cover \\(T_k'\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line in the *shifted* coordinates  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny (it is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Claim.*  Every point \\((a',b)\\in T_k'\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nFor \\((a',b)\\in T_k'\\) we have \\(a',b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=a'-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a'\\ge b\\) implies \\(i\\ge 1\\), and \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ni=a'-b+1\\le (k+1-b)-b+1=k-2b+2\\le k,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Moreover  \n\n\\[\nb = a'-(i-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a',b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).  ∎\n\nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers all points of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Translating back to the original plane\nReplace each \\(\\ell_i\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = (x-(n-k))-(i-1)=x-(n-k+i-1).\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(L_i\\) is the translate of \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  \nConsequently each original point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>n-k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nAll \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); hence they are sunny and distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the whole configuration\n\nCollect the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-k)\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) defined above.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) and is covered by the appropriate vertical line \\(V_a\\), or satisfies \\(a>n-k\\) and, after the shift \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\), belongs to \\(T_k'\\) and is therefore covered by the corresponding sunny line \\(L_i\\).  Hence **all** points of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are not.  Thus the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Extremal values of \\(k\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and they cover \\(T_n\\) (each column is covered by its own vertical line).\n\n* \\(k=n\\):  omit the vertical lines and apply Step 2.3 directly to \\(T_n\\).  \n  The lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y = x-(i-1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n,\n  \\]\n\n  are all sunny and, by the claim proved in Step 2.3, together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus both endpoints \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable, and the construction of Section 2 shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of other values\n\nIn any admissible configuration there are exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence the only possible values are the non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
