ed to each of the \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) longest diagonals.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and let \\(k\\le q\\).  \n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-2k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-2k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \n   After removing the points covered by the vertical lines, the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k^{\\;*}\n   =\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\ a'+b\\le 2k+1\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a copy of \\(T_{2k}\\) shifted right by \\(n-2k\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x-(n-2k+i-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   *Step 4 – verification that the \\(S_i\\) cover \\(T_k^{\\;*}\\).*  \n   Take a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k^{\\;*}\\).  Write \\(a'=a-(n-2k)\\); then \\((a',b)\\in T_{2k}\\) and \\(a'+b\\le 2k+1\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=a'-b+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a',b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b\\le 2k+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le i\\le k .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(b = a-(n-2k+i-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_k^{\\;*}\\) lies on exactly one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Step 5 – total family.*  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-2k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Extrem