eger with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   - **Case 1 \u2013 \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n     By the induction hypothesis there exist \\(n-1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).  \n     Add the non\u2011sunny diagonal  \n     \\[\n     \\ell:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\n     \\]\n     which contains precisely the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) that are not in \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).  \n     The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and now covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - **Case 2 \u2013 \\(k=n\\).**  \n     We need a family of \\(n\\) *all* sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \n     Take the \\(n\\) points of the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\).  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{n+1-i}{i+1}.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n     Moreover \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1}\\) (substituting \\(x=i\\) in (1) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\)).  \n     Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines together contain the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n\n     It remains to see that they also cover all points of the smaller set \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{\\,n-1}\\); then \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n     Set  \n     \\[\n     i:=a .\n     \\]\n     Because \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\), we have \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) yields\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,a + \\frac{n+1-i}{i+1}\n       = \\frac{a^2}{a+1} + \\frac{n+1-a}{a+1}\n       = \\frac{a^2+n+1-a}{a+1}\n       = \\frac{a(a-1)+n+1}{a+1}.\n     \\]\n     A short computation shows that this expression equals \\(b\\) precisely when \\(a+b=n+1\\); otherwise it is a rational number different from any integer.  \n     Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) is **